## The one accepted finding: F1

The two backends disagree about an explicit child ID whose suffix is not numeric.

- SQLite (`internal/storage/sqlite/child_issues.go`, `createChildOnConn`): validates an explicit ID with `IsHierarchicalID(issue.ID)` and checks the derived parent equals `parentID`. `parseChildSuffix` is used only to decide whether to raise the floor. So `p.arm-ace` is accepted as a child of `p`.
- Memory (`internal/storage/memory/memory.go`, `MemoryStorage.CreateChildIssue`, around :1609): gates acceptance on `parseChildSuffixStrict`, which demands an all-digit suffix, so the same ID is rejected with `explicit ID ... is not a child of parent ...`.

Result: `bd create --id p.arm-ace --parent p` succeeds against a database and fails under `bd --no-db`. Molecule-style children (`parent.childref`, see `cmd/bd/template.go:476-505`) are exactly this shape. The plan's before-and-after table promises the `--id` plus `--parent` pair "succeeds when the ID's parent is that parent", with no numeric qualifier, so the memory path contradicts the approved design.

**Fix (the smallest one that closes it):** in `MemoryStorage.CreateChildIssue`, validate an explicit ID by parent match — the ID starts with `parentID + "."` and the remainder is non-empty with no further dot — matching what the SQLite path accepts. Keep `parseChildSuffixStrict` for the counter raise only, exactly as the SQLite path keeps `parseChildSuffix` for the floor raise only. Do not change the SQLite path, and do not change allocation, the duplicate check, the epic-direction check, or anything else.

Add one test case to `internal/storage/memory/child_issues_test.go`: an explicit non-numeric child ID under a parent is accepted by the memory backend and does not disturb the counter, so the next allocated child still comes from the numeric sequence.

